<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parmigianino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Girolamo Francesco Maria Mazzola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parmigianino, born Girolamo Francesco Maria Mazzola in Parma, was a painter and printmaker of the Mannerist style. After working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> he returned to Parma, where he painted frescoes in the church of Santa Maria della Steccata. His works, including the Madonna with the Long Neck and the Self-portrait in a Convex Mirror, are known for their elongated, elegant figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parmigianino.jpg" alt="Parmigianino" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parmigianino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parmigianino_Selfportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
